    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 2 USER]\ne011\tI do not think this is fear of finishing.\ne012\tThe ending makes our main participant sound more certain about leaving her town than she was.\ne013\tWe did not fabricate anything, but Jonah used her strongest lines together and removed hesitation.\ne014\tHe says the whole piece compresses time and that the uncertainty is clear earlier.\ne015\tI feel responsible because I conducted the interviews.\ne016\tThis is not just my taste about pacing.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne017\tThat changes the priority.\ne018\tIf the edit alters how a participant's position is understood, you need to review it against the full interview and the promises you made her, not only against dramatic effect.\ne019\tYou and Jonah may still disagree about how much hesitation belongs in the ending, but the disagreement should be grounded in representation.\ne020\tA focused re-edit of that thread could be necessary even if the larger structure remains.\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne021\tYou moved very quickly to my side.\ne022\tJonah has listened to the full interviews too, and the participant approved a cut three months ago.\ne023\tThe current ending is not identical, but it uses the same recorded material.\ne024\tShe also told us she did not want to supervise every edit.\ne025\tIf I contact her whenever I am uncomfortable, I could be shifting authorship onto her and making Jonah feel I use ethics as a veto.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne026\tThat is fair.\ne027\tApproval and her wish not to manage the edit are real constraints.\ne028\tContacting her is not automatically the respectful option.\ne029\tYou could first compare the approved cut and current ending yourselves, describe the interpretive change without arguing motives, and decide whether it crosses the scope of what she approved.\ne030\tIf you cannot agree, a limited factual or consent question may be better than asking her to adjudicate your artistic conflict.\n[Turn 4 USER]\ne031\tThere is history here.\ne032\tEarly on we said major structural decisions would be mutual.\ne033\tIn practice, Jonah made many of them because he was in the session every day, and I usually trusted him.\ne034\tNow he says I am reopening the structure at the last moment after being less available.\ne035\tThat is partly true.\ne036\tI was reporting another project for two months.\ne037\tBut I also think “you were not in the room” can turn into permanent final say.\n[Turn 4 ASSISTANT]\ne038\tThe informal agreement and the working reality diverged.\ne039\tJonah accumulated practical authorship through sustained editing, while you did not explicitly surrender mutual authority.\ne040\tBoth facts deserve recognition.\ne041\tA fair process now may give greater weight to his knowledge of the cut without treating that as unilateral ownership.\ne042\tThe harder question is what mutual decision means under a deadline: consensus on every change, a defined area of final say, or a rule for what happens when you remain split.",
